Package: clipnest
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
         libglib2.0-bin,
         desktop-file-utils,
         gtk-update-icon-cache,
         pkexec,
         polkitd,
         udev,
         kmod,
         curl
Recommends: clipnest-ocr
Description: clipboard history, snippets, and on-device OCR
 Clipnest watches the clipboard, keeps a searchable history, lets you save
 reusable text snippets, and expand them by keyword anywhere. Clipboard
 history and search work fully on their own, on X11 and Wayland alike. An
 optional GNOME Shell extension (installed per-user by the app itself, not by
 this package — see /usr/share/clipnest/gnome-shell-extension/) adds a picker
 positioned at the pointer and able to appear above fullscreen windows, which
 GTK4 cannot do on its own, plus a compositor-level hotkey.
 .
 100% local: there is no account, no sync, and no analytics. The only
 network traffic this package ever makes is an optional, once-a-day check
 against GitHub's public Releases API for a newer version, toggled off in
 Settings.
 .
 Install clipnest-ocr as well (the default — this package only
 Recommends it, so `apt install --no-install-recommends clipnest` skips
 it for a lighter install) to recognize text in copied images, entirely
 on-device.
 .
 Known limitation on Ubuntu 22.04 (and any system whose GTK 4 runtime
 predates 4.10): a rare upstream GTK bug can make Clipnest quit
 unexpectedly if another running application interacts with the clipboard
 in an unusual way. This is a bug in the system's GTK library, not in
 Clipnest, already fixed upstream in GTK 4.10 — Ubuntu 24.04 and newer are
 unaffected, and clipboard history is never lost. Clipnest detects this at
 startup and explains it under Settings > Permissions; see
 debian/README.source's "Known gap #5" in this package's source for the
 full root cause.

Package: clipnest-ocr
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, clipnest-ocr-data (= ${source:Version})
Description: on-device OCR runtime for Clipnest
 Vendored ONNX Runtime (CPU inference only, MIT-licensed — see
 /usr/share/doc/clipnest-ocr/copyright) that Clipnest uses to recognize
 text in copied images entirely on-device. Nothing in this package ever
 makes a network request or downloads a model at runtime.
 .
 Split out from the clipnest package (which only Recommends this one) so
 that installing without OCR support needs no vendored native library at
 all.

Package: clipnest-ocr-data
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: PP-OCRv5 model data for Clipnest's on-device OCR
 The detection, recognition, and text-line orientation ONNX models (and
 character dictionary) clipnest-ocr's vendored ONNX Runtime loads to
 recognize text in copied images. Architecture-independent data only — no
 executable code. Nothing here is ever fetched or updated over the network
 by this package; models are pinned to the version they were built and
 tested against.
